== Synopsis ==
As revealed in ''[[Chapter I]]: Act VIII - [[To the Shore's End]]'', The Lament disrupts the fundamental laws of physics, with gravity remaining the only consistent force. This destabilization causes reality to fracture unpredictably, giving rise to anomalies that affect both the natural world and human infrastructure. These phenomena range from warped terrains to time-displaced echoes of past lives, driven by accumulated [[Remnant|Remnant Energy]]—data-like residues of memory and emotion.

During the quest, ''[[Legacy of the Lasting Night]],'' the emotional manifestation of the Lament involves psychological disruptions in affected individuals. It induces symptoms such as obsessive behavior, hallucinations, and emotional instability—particularly despair, grief, and fixation on past traumas. These effects are believed to stem from Remnant Energy interacting with human consciousness, creating feedback loops of unresolved emotion.

Following the onset of the Lament, all forms of exploration beyond Solaris-3 were suspended. The destabilization of physical laws—leaving gravity as the only constant—rendered long-range travel and advanced technologies unreliable. Interstellar expansion, once a symbol of human ambition, came to a complete halt as foundational systems failed under unpredictable conditions.

Despite these limitations, certain scholars and explorers continued their research, driven by lingering curiosity and a desire to return to the skies. While official efforts ceased, isolated attempts persist in hopes of understanding the Lament’s origins and reclaiming what was lost.

During the exploration across Honami City in the questline, [[Stagnant Dawn on Wastelands|''Stagnant Dawn on Wastelands'']], observation shows how the Lament caused a mass chaotic effect across Honami City's reality. Space itself had been sliced through the effects of the Iridglow, capable of cutting through and confusing space, making it difficult to reach the source of it, Neon Tower, where the Lament resides. Through Chisa and Sumika’s time in the city, they explain that the Lament had consumed the entire location (starting from Neon Tower) and entrapped the city in a mass Sonoro Sphere which subjected the region onto a unusual time loop. Time itself has also been affected greatly where those inside experience a time dilation; months could pass within the interior while years pass outside of it.  Over time, the Lament is stated to have weakened and is no longer the real thing, it's power fading, though Chisa attests that it's still dangerous. More so when the Fractsidus begun experimenting on the land since the space in Honami City was considered to be like shattered glass, where anything too forceful could make it collapse.

While Honami City had been originally sealed, through the combination of [[Sumika]] cracking it open by sending out a message from the gathered Longings, it causes the restraints around the Sonoro to weaken, allowing the Black Shores and Abby to slowly cause the connection between the city and the outside world to become stronger. After the defeat of the Chronorift Yakujin, the loops ended with the Black Shores making a full connection with the city, allowing time flow to stabilize within the Sonoro itself. Though hazard zones and dangers do still remain in spite of the situation being settled.

When reading [[Sumika's Notes]], who catalogued some of the effects the Lament had on Honami, she wrote down that everything two-dimensional, three dimensional, is equally sliced by the Lament like it does with people, buildings, and everything else within the space, where Namipon adds their own existence was torn apart from it. Plants themselves aren’t bound by the “reset” though, unaffected by the way time moves forward and backward within the fixed range of the temporal loops. Food became compressed Remnant Energy with living things no longer feeling hunger. One fortunate consequence of the unusual timeflow in Honami City is that in spite of Chisa being in danger of Overclocking, the Sonoro Sphere stalled Chisa’s condition and prevented her from endangering herself. Neon Tower, as it is the source and center of the Lament in Honami City, and is the one place where if the right path is found, can allow one to escape the city. To reach the source of it, however, one needs filtered Longings to make way or risk finding themself lost from the rearranged space where time moves slower the closer one gets to the tower. This is because time is completely out of sync, causing Sumika to see time fragments of her past itself. Only through the Longings can one find the right path as they resonate with the source of the Lament. 

While the Lament cannot be controlled or manipulated in normal circumstances, [[Stranger Things in Honami|''Stranger Things in Honami'']] shows how the Left Behind known as Miki’s father managed to utilize the Lament’s powers due to being devoured and becoming a part of the phenomenon. Namipon explains that Longings are frequencies that persist within the Lament, not disappearing even when devoured, which could in turn affect the Lament’s residual power in Honami if the Longings are strong enough. Though Miki’s father was unable to properly control the power of the Lament, which in turn twisted the wishes and was only stopped when he found satisfaction that Shashou Cafe was doing alright.

==Trivia==
* The third storyline reveals that gravity was the one law that was unaltered.
* The term ''"The Lament"'' is officially translated across multiple languages with words that convey cries, screams, or expressions of mourning, emphasizing its identity as both a world-ending catastrophe and a resonant sound event. In Simplified and Traditional Chinese, it appears as 悲鸣 / 悲鳴; in Japanese, as 悲鳴 (''himei''); in Korean, as 비명 (''bimyeong''); in French, as ''La Lamentation''; in German, as ''Lamento''; and in Spanish, as ''Lamento''. This linguistic consistency aligns with ''Wuthering Waves’'' broader use of sound-centric terminology underscoring a thematic framework where sonic and emotional frequencies shape the metaphysics of the world.
* The Shorekeeper describes the Lament as ''“the death knell for humanity, leaving billions upon billions of lives lost to its wail.”'' The phrase ''death knell'' refers not only to a metaphorical warning of imminent demise, but also to an actual tolling of a bell traditionally used to signal death in many cultures.